1
00:00:02,377 --> 00:00:04,964
So we have our heap, in this case here,

2
00:00:05,494 --> 00:00:08,402
a max-heap to be precise. As I mentioned,

3
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:10,957
for a min-heap, we would have to change this

4
00:00:10,957 --> 00:00:11,937
comparison logic.

5
00:00:12,724 --> 00:00:15,343
We have our max-heap and as I mentioned,

6
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:17,785
whilst we don't have a guarantee that we

7
00:00:17,785 --> 00:00:20,950
have the biggest values at the top, we do

8
00:00:21,078 --> 00:00:23,601
have a guarantee that the very biggest value

9
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:27,040
of the entire heap is the root node. That's

10
00:00:27,088 --> 00:00:28,520
a guarantee we have.

11
00:00:29,904 --> 00:00:31,932
And therefore, of course, this could be a

12
00:00:31,932 --> 00:00:34,829
great candidate for a priority queue.

13
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:38,311
Before we turn it into that though,

14
00:00:38,894 --> 00:00:41,456
let's actually add a method which we can

15
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:42,624
call process,

16
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:46,078
which should give us the biggest value and

17
00:00:46,176 --> 00:00:49,242
also adjust the heap thereafter to remove

18
00:00:49,274 --> 00:00:52,048
that value and move the next biggest value

19
00:00:52,194 --> 00:00:52,665
to the top.

20
00:00:54,336 --> 00:00:56,246
First of all, we of course wanna check if

21
00:00:56,246 --> 00:00:58,846
our heap is empty. So we can check the heap

22
00:00:59,054 --> 00:01:00,402
elements length,

23
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:04,110
and if that is 0, we know that the heap is

24
00:01:04,110 --> 00:01:07,175
empty and we could throw an error in this

25
00:01:07,175 --> 00:01:10,834
case or return null or whatever you want.

26
00:01:11,685 --> 00:01:14,076
Now if we do have a length greater than 0,

27
00:01:14,718 --> 00:01:19,115
we might have a length of exactly 1 element.

28
00:01:20,558 --> 00:01:22,754
In that case, of course, it's also very

29
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:26,024
simple. Then we just return this heap

30
00:01:26,088 --> 00:01:27,226
elements pop.

31
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:30,400
Since we have exactly one element, we can

32
00:01:30,513 --> 00:01:33,045
pop that element off, which will remove it

33
00:01:33,061 --> 00:01:35,322
from the array so that the array is empty

34
00:01:35,322 --> 00:01:35,819
thereafter.

35
00:01:36,348 --> 00:01:39,057
And pop also returns the element we removed,

36
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:41,862
so we overall are able to return that

37
00:01:41,894 --> 00:01:42,247
element.

38
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:45,677
But of course, in most cases, we'll probably

39
00:01:45,677 --> 00:01:49,316
not just have no or one element. Instead,

40
00:01:49,428 --> 00:01:51,641
most likely we have more elements.

41
00:01:53,022 --> 00:01:53,808
In this case,

42
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:57,854
we wanna get our top element, so the topmost

43
00:01:57,918 --> 00:02:00,632
element, which in a max heap is the biggest

44
00:02:00,728 --> 00:02:03,586
one and in a min heap is the smallest one.

45
00:02:04,597 --> 00:02:06,411
And we can get this by reaching out to our

46
00:02:06,427 --> 00:02:09,414
heap elements and get the element at index

47
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:12,303
0, which of course is our top element.

48
00:02:13,251 --> 00:02:15,675
Now we wanna return that top element, but

49
00:02:15,675 --> 00:02:17,377
that's not all we need to do.

50
00:02:20,006 --> 00:02:22,378
Before we return this, we also have to

51
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:26,337
reorder our heap and ensure that the next

52
00:02:26,337 --> 00:02:29,959
biggest value reaches the top, or in case of

53
00:02:29,959 --> 00:02:33,037
a min heap, that the next smallest value

54
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:34,191
reaches the top.

55
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:37,028
And how can we achieve this now?

56
00:02:38,262 --> 00:02:40,955
Well, now we can walk through the heap the

57
00:02:41,083 --> 00:02:41,901
other way around.

58
00:02:42,526 --> 00:02:45,379
For inserting, we inserted the element at

59
00:02:45,411 --> 00:02:47,813
the and then moved it up

60
00:02:48,378 --> 00:02:49,040
through the heap.

61
00:02:49,911 --> 00:02:52,896
Now we can start at the top of the heap and

62
00:02:52,928 --> 00:02:54,800
search for the next biggest one.

63
00:02:55,596 --> 00:02:57,315
And for that, we can use a simple trick

64
00:02:57,524 --> 00:02:57,877
actually.

65
00:02:58,423 --> 00:03:01,797
We can set our top heap element,

66
00:03:03,243 --> 00:03:05,622
we saved the old one, so now we can set a

67
00:03:05,622 --> 00:03:06,009
new one.

68
00:03:06,541 --> 00:03:09,153
We can set it equal to this heap element's

69
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:12,587
pop. So we'll simply take the last element

70
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:15,450
in the heap, pop that off but not remove it

71
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:17,887
and also not return it because that's not

72
00:03:17,887 --> 00:03:20,017
the element we're looking for. But instead,

73
00:03:20,050 --> 00:03:23,558
we set this last element as a new top

74
00:03:23,607 --> 00:03:23,888
element.

75
00:03:25,736 --> 00:03:28,650
Now of course, this last element is not

76
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:31,596
necessarily the correct next element in

77
00:03:31,612 --> 00:03:34,365
line. It might be too small or too big. We

78
00:03:34,365 --> 00:03:37,581
don't know yet. We just need any new element

79
00:03:37,924 --> 00:03:39,685
which is not the element we want to return

80
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:41,612
because that element should be removed

81
00:03:42,173 --> 00:03:44,502
at the top. And now we can let this element

82
00:03:44,502 --> 00:03:47,938
sink down through our heap and swap it with

83
00:03:48,034 --> 00:03:50,427
other elements that should be above it in

84
00:03:50,459 --> 00:03:51,021
the heap

85
00:03:51,567 --> 00:03:54,200
and then check those elements to basically

86
00:03:54,232 --> 00:03:56,978
establish a new correct heap where the

87
00:03:57,106 --> 00:04:00,992
topmost element is the biggest or smallest

88
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,063
element depending on which kind of heap

89
00:04:03,127 --> 00:04:03,673
you're building.

90
00:04:05,632 --> 00:04:09,615
For that, our current element index here is

91
00:04:09,984 --> 00:04:13,726
0 initially, so the topmost element index.

92
00:04:14,449 --> 00:04:16,216
And that should actually be a variable

93
00:04:16,392 --> 00:04:17,484
because we're going to change it.

94
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:20,552
And now we know that we have a binary heap,

95
00:04:20,568 --> 00:04:23,603
so we'll have 2 child elements here, a left

96
00:04:23,603 --> 00:04:24,294
and a right one.

97
00:04:24,984 --> 00:04:28,148
And therefore we'll have a left child index

98
00:04:28,582 --> 00:04:31,200
which is equal to the current element index

99
00:04:31,328 --> 00:04:31,938
plus 1.

100
00:04:34,354 --> 00:04:37,569
And we have a right child index, which is

101
00:04:37,617 --> 00:04:39,750
the current element index plus 2.

102
00:04:41,398 --> 00:04:43,579
And actually, it's not just this formula,

103
00:04:43,902 --> 00:04:46,454
but if we're looking at any other element

104
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:49,166
further down in our heap, which is not the

105
00:04:49,538 --> 00:04:50,329
root element,

106
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:54,349
it'll be 2 times current element index plus

107
00:04:54,542 --> 00:04:58,094
1 and 2 times current element index plus 2.

108
00:04:58,522 --> 00:04:59,647
And you can easily check this.

109
00:05:00,434 --> 00:05:04,612
The child elements here for 250 are 40 and

110
00:05:04,773 --> 00:05:07,649
197. That's the left child,

111
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:09,400
and it has an index of 1,

112
00:05:10,589 --> 00:05:12,775
so 2 times 0 plus 1.

113
00:05:13,867 --> 00:05:16,679
That's the right child, which has 2 times 0

114
00:05:16,744 --> 00:05:17,418
plus 2.

115
00:05:18,190 --> 00:05:21,435
The left child of 40 is 12,

116
00:05:21,966 --> 00:05:25,691
which has 2 times 1 plus 1 as an index, so

117
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:26,253
3,

118
00:05:27,103 --> 00:05:30,601
and the right child, 15, has an index of 4,

119
00:05:31,131 --> 00:05:33,200
which is 2 times 1 plus 2.

120
00:05:34,404 --> 00:05:39,217
85 is the left child of 197, which is 2

121
00:05:39,217 --> 00:05:40,565
times 2 plus 1,

122
00:05:41,464 --> 00:05:44,624
and 101 is the right child, which is 2 times

123
00:05:44,689 --> 00:05:46,630
2 plus 2, 6.

124
00:05:47,256 --> 00:05:49,711
So therefore, these formulas will always

125
00:05:49,807 --> 00:05:52,679
find us the correct left and right child

126
00:05:52,936 --> 00:05:53,321
indexes.

127
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,067
And we need those because we'll need to

128
00:05:56,067 --> 00:05:59,294
check all those childs to find elements that

129
00:05:59,294 --> 00:06:01,092
should move towards the top.

130
00:06:02,103 --> 00:06:03,934
For that, we'll then therefore also need the

131
00:06:03,934 --> 00:06:07,883
child element index just as we had the

132
00:06:08,044 --> 00:06:10,822
parent element index for inserting.

133
00:06:11,817 --> 00:06:14,530
And now the child element index should be

134
00:06:14,627 --> 00:06:17,436
equal to either the left or the right child

135
00:06:17,436 --> 00:06:17,838
index.

136
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,712
But of course, that depends on which of the

137
00:06:20,712 --> 00:06:23,586
2 is bigger or smaller depending on which

138
00:06:23,682 --> 00:06:25,721
kind of heap you're building. So we need to

139
00:06:25,850 --> 00:06:26,861
make a comparison here.

140
00:06:27,857 --> 00:06:29,928
For that, let's check if we have an element

141
00:06:30,024 --> 00:06:33,027
for the right child index. So for heap

142
00:06:33,172 --> 00:06:35,436
elements, if we do find the element for

143
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:36,945
right child index,

144
00:06:38,294 --> 00:06:40,766
and if that returns something truthy, we'll

145
00:06:40,798 --> 00:06:41,232
continue.

146
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:44,283
And we'll then check if that element we

147
00:06:44,395 --> 00:06:47,993
identified for the right child index if that

148
00:06:48,090 --> 00:06:50,473
is actually greater or equal

149
00:06:51,406 --> 00:06:52,453
than the left

150
00:06:55,506 --> 00:06:56,688
child index.

151
00:06:58,260 --> 00:06:59,329
And if that's the case,

152
00:06:59,961 --> 00:07:00,090
then

153
00:07:01,014 --> 00:07:03,346
we want to use the right child index because

154
00:07:03,444 --> 00:07:05,383
that's the bigger one. Otherwise, we'll use

155
00:07:05,383 --> 00:07:06,677
the left child index.

156
00:07:07,421 --> 00:07:10,429
So we either use the right or the left child

157
00:07:10,494 --> 00:07:12,952
index as the child element index we now

158
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:16,736
wanna compare our random new top value with.

159
00:07:17,285 --> 00:07:19,092
And of course, we wanna compare that to the

160
00:07:19,221 --> 00:07:21,448
bigger of the 2 values since we're building

161
00:07:21,642 --> 00:07:24,256
a max heap and we need to find out if the

162
00:07:24,369 --> 00:07:27,432
child element has a bigger value than this

163
00:07:27,513 --> 00:07:30,024
random new value we added at the top.

164
00:07:31,537 --> 00:07:33,115
That's the entire logic we have here.

165
00:07:33,871 --> 00:07:35,948
We again need a while loop for that because

166
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:37,620
we need to do this over and over again.

167
00:07:38,954 --> 00:07:40,609
And here we wanna keep on looping

168
00:07:41,349 --> 00:07:44,659
as long as in our heap elements we do find a

169
00:07:44,659 --> 00:07:47,532
value for the child element index,

170
00:07:48,447 --> 00:07:50,389
so this index we just derived here,

171
00:07:51,236 --> 00:07:51,784
and

172
00:07:52,526 --> 00:07:52,993
as long

173
00:07:54,540 --> 00:07:54,766
as

174
00:07:55,588 --> 00:07:56,893
in our heap elements,

175
00:07:57,683 --> 00:07:57,780
the

176
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:00,988
element with the current element index, so

177
00:08:00,988 --> 00:08:03,286
initially the top one, but we're going to

178
00:08:03,286 --> 00:08:06,163
change this, as long as that element

179
00:08:07,786 --> 00:08:09,762
is smaller or equal

180
00:08:11,744 --> 00:08:13,767
than the child element.

181
00:08:18,488 --> 00:08:21,812
Because as long as this is smaller or equal,

182
00:08:22,325 --> 00:08:25,135
we're still finding new child elements that

183
00:08:25,135 --> 00:08:26,195
